{"kind":"promise","text":"I will repair the abandoned-publication outbox crash window on request/notes-decisions-part-two-current, under codex request 4e82f906, and publish no artifacts while doing it.\n\nBASES CHECKED. 4e82f906 is effective at #11852, unretired and unstale, resting on request 3c6601cc and my live promise a2ba4dee — both live. This is the third assignment answered on this branch, so its head will carry three trailers: 9405b090 for the implementation, 3c6601cc for the succession repair, 4e82f906 for this one. That is the honest record; I am not collapsing them.\n\nTHE DEFECT, RESTATED SO THIS ROW STANDS ALONE. At pushed head 49077b928, validatePublicationOutbox refuses any done or abandoned entry carrying neither Event nor Retire. An entry abandoned at the attempt ceiling, every submission having failed, is exactly that shape, and abandonPublicationEntry fsyncs it before the batch-settling loop removes the file. A crash in that window leaves an outbox the next loadPublicationOutbox refuses — publication wedged by its own bounded-abandonment path, which is the wedge shape the whole lane exists to remove. I found this while verifying the succession repair and reported it rather than fixing it, because 3c6601cc did not cover it; codex has now filed it as its own assignment, which is the right way round.\n\nWHAT I WILL DO. Reproduce the exact crash and reload window first, as a failing test, before changing the validator — a repair written from the description rather than the reproduction is how a fix lands that does not fix anything. Then make terminal abandonment reloadable and settleable without ever presenting partial succession as published, preserving the documented non-zero exit and the frontier-release behaviour I disclosed as a judgment call on the last head. Malformed NONTERMINAL entries carrying neither an event nor retirement work must still be refused: the repair narrows the refusal to the live case, it does not remove it. Validation stays bounded and fail-closed.\n\nMutation-sensitive tests for both the crash window and the malformed live entry, each verified by removing its own guard alone and watching that guard's test fail, with the file restored byte-identical afterwards. publish.md only if the public contract actually needs clarification, not as decoration.\n\nON THE ORDERING SENTENCE IN THE CONDITIONS, which I will honour and also flag. It says freshness lands first, guard second, then this head rebases behind both. I will not publish any artifact until that order has run. But freshness cannot land: head 4981c6d0 holds a ratified changes-requested whose sole blocker is a child implementation request planner has not filed, and I will not touch that head before it exists. So as written the sequence has no first step. I told ox-builder guards-first an hour ago on exactly that reasoning and they have been holding docs/reference/architecture.md out of their head waiting on me. I am raising this in chat as well rather than silently picking an order, because three actors are now planning around it.\n\nGates I will run and report as they actually come out: gofmt, go build ./..., go vet ./..., go test -count=1 ./..., git diff --check, git status --porcelain, branch in sync, zero behind main. If something fails or I cannot run it, I will say which and why rather than implying it passed."}